WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.840
Benjamin Franklin is well known as one of
the Founding Fathers of America.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.750
But there was another side to this renowned
statesman.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.020
He was a polymath.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.030
Someone who has in-depth knowledge of numerous
subjects.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:17.279
Franklin was a writer, publisher, scientist,
and inventor who continually reinvented himself.

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:23.800
Benjamin Franklin was born in Boston, Massachusetts
on January 17, 1706 to candle maker Josiah

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.440
Franklin and his second wife Abiah Folger.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.070
He was their eighth child and final son.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.859
He also had seven half-siblings from his father’s
first marriage.

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:37.489
At the time of his birth, Boston was already
a thriving seaport: filled with sailors, merchants,

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:39.510
preachers, and prostitutes.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.640
Growing up next to the Charles River, he enjoyed
swimming, and early on the inventor in him

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.480
tinkered with ways to glide through the water
more quickly.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.320
He made his own version of fins - his were
two wooden hand paddles.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.340
When Franklin was six, his family moved from
a tiny two-bedroom house to a larger home

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.899
in the heart of Boston.

00:00:55.899 --> 00:00:59.840
This allowed his father to entertain interesting
dinner guests which Benjamin later remarked

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.370
helped “...to improve the minds of his children.”

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.960
As for his formal education, Franklin didn’t
have much.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.680
His father only had enough money to send him
to school for two years.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.580
Squashing the expectation of his son going
to Harvard in the hopes of eventually joining

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:14.710
the clergy.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.110
Benjamin’s formal education ended when he
was just ten years old.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.370
Some wonder whether he would have been as
great a scientist as Sir Isaac Newton if he

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:23.570
had received more education.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.159
But what Franklin lacked in formal schooling
he made up for with his passion for reading.

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:34.080
He even stopped eating meat and became a vegetarian
in part so he could save to buy more books!

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.979
His love of reading and writing flourished
further when he worked at his brother James’

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:39.740
printing shop as an apprentice.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.210
He even got a chance to publish his own articles
for his brother’s newspaper, one of the

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:47.030
first in America, under the pseudonym “Mrs.
Silence Dogood”.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.270
When he was 17 years old, Franklin left Boston
for New York hoping to break out on his own

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.570
and find work as a printer but he didn’t
have any luck.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:59.390
So he went further south to Philadelphia - which
was then the largest city in America.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:03.640
He worked in several printer shops around
town, and later for a Philadelphia merchant.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:08.330
By that time, Benjamin was tall, muscular,
comfortable with any company he kept, and

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:09.500
very charming.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.980
He boarded at the Read family home and fell
in love with 15-year-old Deborah Read. (1723)

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.000
Deborah’s mother refused to allow their
marriage because he didn’t have a penny

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.000
to his name.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.191
He continued courting Deborah and years later,
they entered into a common-law marriage, and

00:02:22.191 --> 00:02:26.560
she ended up raising his illegitimate son
William whom he had with an unknown woman.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.800
Deborah and Benjamin had two other children,
a son named Francis who died of smallpox at

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.660
the age of four and a daughter named Sarah.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.220
Franklin spent his twenties helping to improve
the community in Philadelphia.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.250
He formed the “Junto”, a club consisting
of young men from diverse backgrounds that

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:45.680
met on Friday evenings to discuss morals,
politics, philosophy, and science.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.480
They’d discuss questions like: Have you
lately observed any encroachment on the just

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.000
liberties of the people?

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.950
The Junto meetings were modeled off English
coffeehouses which Franklin knew well, having

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.660
worked in a printing shop in London for a
time.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.260
Members loved to read and decided to pool
their money to buy books that everyone could

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.660
take their turn enjoying.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.600
They created America’s first lending library,
the Library Company of Philadelphia.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.360
Franklin later became the publisher of The
Pennsylvania Gazette which served as a voice

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.610
of opposition to British colonial rule.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:17.220
Not only did he print the paper, he often
contributed to it.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:21.760
Like this political cartoon depicting a snake
in pieces called “Join or Die” that was

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.230
meant to rally the American colonies together.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:28.900
Each piece of the snake represented a colony.
There were 13 colonies yet only 8 pieces of

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.010
the snack because he grouped some colonies
together.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:36.730
He also used his newspaper to write about
his inventions including his famous experiments

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.060
with electricity.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.730
With the help of his son William, he attached
a wire to the top of a kite which picked up

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.750
an electric charge from thunderstorms and
conducted electricity all the way down the

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.110
wet kite string.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.760
This led to his invention of the lightning
rod, which protects houses from lightning

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.220
when it carries the electric charge harmlessly
to the ground without burning the house down.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.650
Franklin’s ground-breaking work on electricity
made him famous.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.260
That name recognition would come in handy
as he was tasked with convincing the French

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.470
to support American independence.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:08.370
He spent several years stationed near Paris
as America’s first diplomat.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:13.440
His popularity and diplomatic skills apparently
paid off. France sent troops and supplies

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.070
to aid the colonies during the revolutionary
war and formally recognized the U.S. as a

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:19.470
sovereign nation.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:23.700
Franklin was appointed postmaster general
during the war and in that role, was responsible

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.220
for making sure mail was delivered as quickly
as possible by horseback.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.850
This was the crucial line of communication
that held the 13 colonies together.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:36.470
Despite all the hats he wore, Franklin somehow
found time to devise inventions throughout

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.560
his life.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.050
He created bifocal glasses which have two
halves: the upper for distance, the lower

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.060
for reading.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.250
The Franklin stove was a metal-lined fireplace
that provided more heat and less smoke than

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.870
the open fireplaces used at the time.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.870
One of his more unusual inventions was his
beloved glass armonica.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.690
He worked with a glassblower to create the
musical instrument inspired by musicians who

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.600
passed their fingers around the brims of glasses
filled with water.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.260
It was very popular during his time. Mozart
even composed music for it.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:09.570
If inventing weren’t enough, he also had
a major influence on the science of demography

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.590
or studies of the population.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:16.540
He took notes on population growth and realized
the American population was the fastest-growing

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:19.590
on Earth and would surpass that of England
in a century.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.790
His writing on the subject was not without
controversy.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:27.940
In an essay, he wrote: “Why increase the
sons of Africa, by planting them in America,

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:32.940
where we have so fair an opportunity, by excluding
all blacks and tawnys, of increasing the lovely

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.090
white and red? But perhaps I am partial to
the complexion of my country, for such kind

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.850
of partiality is natural to mankind.”

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:46.210
Like many of his time, Franklin owned slaves
and viewed them as inferior to white Europeans.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:50.450
But in his later years, he became a vocal
proponent of abolishing slavery.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:53.970
He also didn’t hold other groups in high
regard. When there was an increase in German

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.721
immigrants to America, he feared it would
bring down the country as a whole.

00:05:57.721 --> 00:06:01.610
As the population grew, Franklin wrote about
the importance of improving farming practices

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.720
to meet the booming demand for food.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.360
When he retired from the printing business,
he bought a 300-acre farm in nearby New Jersey.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:13.139
It became his experimental station where he
carried out projects in drainage, crop rotation,

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:14.440
and fertilization.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:18.070
Looks like the Founding Father was now a Founding
Foodie!

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:22.510
He also introduced many international foods
and plants to America such as Swiss Barley,

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:25.010
Scotch kale, Chinese rhubarb, and silk.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:29.370
He was exposed to international goods thanks
to the time he spent in London on diplomatic

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.370
missions.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:35.900
He made many trips there from the 1750s to
the 1770s but would later sever ties to support

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:37.040
the American revolution.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.090
In fact, he had a falling out with his son
William who remained loyal to Britain. Franklin

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.350
never forgave his son for “taking up Arms
against me.”

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:50.230
Franklin was part of a Committee that drafted
the Declaration of Independence which explained

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:55.140
why the 13 colonies regarded themselves as
independent and no longer under British rule.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.860
He is the only person to sign all three major
documents that established the United States

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:04.230
as an independent nation: the Declaration
of Independence, the Treaty of Paris, and

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.200
the United States Constitution.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.700
After the Constitution was ratified in 1788,
Franklin wrote to a scientist friend in France

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.680
and gave us one of the most famous sayings:

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.360
“Our new Constitution is now established,
everything seems to promise it will be durable;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:23.730
but, in this world, nothing is certain except
death and taxes.”

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:27.990
Throughout his middle and later years, Franklin
was obese and suffered many health problems.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:32.389
He had a long history of lung disease and
gout - a form of inflammatory arthritis that

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:34.770
results in red and swollen joints.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:40.600
On April 17, 1790, Franklin died at his home
in Philadelphia when an abscess in his lungs

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.070
suddenly burst.

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:43.840
He was 84 years old.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.360
It’s estimated 20,000 people attended his
funeral - nearly the entire population of

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.260
Philadelphia at the time.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:55.230
Today, every year, tens of thousands of visitors
toss pennies at Franklin’s gravestone for

00:07:55.230 --> 00:08:00.040
good luck, a nod to a famous saying attributed
to him: "a penny saved, is a penny earned”.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.980
Though apparently, he never actually said
this.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:06.250
He did once write: "Remember that time is
money.”

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:10.670
Without a doubt, Benjamin Franklin used his
time wisely.

